18. Security Requirements

Implement the following protections.

Authentication
Validate Supabase access tokens on protected backend routes.
Reject missing, invalid, or expired tokens.
Do not trust user IDs supplied by clients.
Enforce ownership using RLS and server-side authorization.
Handle logout and expired sessions correctly.
Secrets
Keep Gemini and Supabase service-role credentials server-side.
Never commit .env files containing real secrets.
Provide a safe .env.example.
Do not log credentials.
Input Security
Validate all API inputs.
Limit request body sizes.
Reject unsupported protocols in URLs.
Escape or safely render user-provided content.
Avoid unsafe HTML rendering.
Protect against SQL injection by using parameterized queries and safe query builders.
Protect against cross-site scripting.
Apply appropriate CORS restrictions.
External Links and URL Handling
Permit only HTTP and HTTPS URLs.
Use safe external-link attributes.
Do not automatically fetch arbitrary user URLs without SSRF defenses.
If URL previews are supported, block private, loopback, link-local, and internal network addresses, and validate every redirect.
API Protection
Apply rate limits to authentication-adjacent and AI endpoints.
Apply stricter limits to expensive AI operations.
Use centralized error handling.
Avoid leaking internal error details.
Use sensible timeouts.
Avoid unbounded retries.
Data Privacy
Process only the minimum data required for AI requests.
Avoid storing unnecessary private content in logs.
Clearly indicate when content is sent to Gemini for analysis.
Do not send user data to analytics or external services without an implemented, disclosed reason.
Production Configuration
Configure HTTPS at deployment.
Set appropriate security headers.
Restrict CORS to the intended frontend origin.
Keep dependencies current and avoid abandoned packages where practical.
